WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.740
Every C++ object has a lifetime.

2
00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:10.680
A local object normally begins when execution reaches its declaration and ends when its scope exits.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:17.580
A heap object begins when it is created through an allocation mechanism and ends when the owning code releases it.

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:26.960
Temporaries, references, pointers, constructor work, destructor work, and move operations all participate in this lifecycle.

5
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:33.860
Memory safety depends on code using an object only while that object is alive and in a valid state.

6
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:40.140
Ownership describes who is responsible for ending that lifetime or releasing the resource behind the object.

7
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.200
Owning access carries cleanup responsibility.

8
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.780
Borrowed access uses an object without controlling how long it lives.

9
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:56.680
A reference parameter, a raw pointer used for observation, or a view into a buffer may all be appropriate borrowed access when the lifetime rule is obvious.

10
00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:05.180
They become risky when the borrower stores the access, passes it into asynchronous work, or assumes the owner will remain available without a clear contract.

11
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.660
Unclear ownership creates recurring defects.

12
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.160
Two owners may both attempt cleanup.

13
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.100
No owner may clean up at all.

14
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.880
Code may keep a pointer after the target has been destroyed, or use an object after ownership was transferred.

15
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.940
These bugs are hard to review because the relevant decision may be far from the failing line of code.

16
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.440
Good interfaces make ownership visible.

17
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.300
Return owning objects when a caller receives responsibility.

18
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.440
Use references or clearly non-owning pointers for short-lived access.

19
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:41.780
Avoid APIs where callers must guess whether they should delete, retain, or merely inspect a value.

20
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:49.960
In professional C++, lifetime rules should be easy to explain during review and hard to misuse in ordinary maintenance.

21
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:58.160
A useful review habit is to trace creation, transfer, borrowing, and destruction as one story.

22
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:04.900
If that story requires private knowledge of distant code, the design is probably asking future maintainers to carry too much risk.
